1:As I wake naturally on this cozy Sunday morning, I habitually open CatChat to see what everyone has posted.
2:player
3:{character:player}...Kana is preparing for her new album, Miki is cooking up something strange again, and Nadeshiko is organizing a draw... What? Organizing a draw?
4:My fingertip pauses while swiping the screen. Nadeshiko's latest CatChat post has caught my attention: "Online draw with a mysterious prize for one lucky winner who doesn't know how to drive a motorcycle and has free time. Reply in the comments below to participate in this event. I reserve the right over the final interpretation."
5:As a human, I'm naturally attracted to lottery activities, but what really makes me curious is that Nadeshiko, a motorcycle expert, is organizing a draw and specifying that only beginners who can't drive a motorcycle can participate.
6:I quickly tap the reply box and enter my situation—
7:Although I don't know what the prize is, I do fit the criteria, so obliviously I have to sign up and try my luck.
9:*Beep* —The moment I walk outside the door, I hear the horn of a motorcycle close to me. The moment I turn to look, a heavy motorcycle stops right next to me.
10:Nadeshiko
11:{character:Nadeshiko}Happy weekend, {var:player_nickname}.
12:The woman riding the motorcycle pushes up the windshield of her helmet, revealing a pair of sharp and bright eyes, then looks at me and starts speaking.
13:{character:player}...Nadeshiko?
14:{character:Nadeshiko}Surprised to see me? It seems that you didn't come out to greet me after receiving the notification that you won the prize.
15:{character:player}Huh? I won the prize???
17:{character:player}!!!
18:{character:player}Sorry, I didn't check my messages. I actually won?!
19:{character:Nadeshiko}Bingo! Let me see... Not bad, put on a helmet and hop on behind me.
20: Straddling the motorcycle, Nadeshiko looks at me and seems very satisfied. I try my best to ignore the feeling of being a lamb being led to the slaughter. Hugging the helmet she threw to me, I eagerly ask her questions out of confusion.
21:{character:player}So the mysterious prize is a ride on the motorcycle with you, Nadeshiko?
23:{character:Nadeshiko}Ahem, something like that. Anyway, it's a very worthwhile arrangement, and you'll be happy with it.
24:There's nothing to hesitate about then! I quickly wear the helmet and get on the motorcycle.
25:{character:Nadeshiko}Hug me.
26:{character:player}That doesn't seem appropriate... Ah!
27:The moment the motorcycle starts up, I have no choice but to wrap my arms around Nadeshiko's waist under the intimidation of inertia.
28:The motorcycle roars as it shuttles through the intersecting streets, taking several twists and turns before we arrive at the destination. I get off in a daze and see a long line of plain buildings instead of the beautiful lush suburban scenery I was expecting. I read out the sign in front of the buildings.
29:{character:player}..."One Han City Motorcycle For Life Training Base." This is... a motorcycle driving school?
30:{character:Nadeshiko}That's right! {var:player_nickname}, congratulations on winning the prize. You get to be the first student of "Nadeshiko's Motorcycle License Test Camp."
31:{character:player}...Motorcycle license test?
32:I'm so confused. This prize seems to be something I... never expected.
33:I finally understand what's happening after we enter the driving school and Nadeshiko explains everything to me.
34:Nadeshiko accidentally exceeded her living expense budget because she bought a collector's limited-edition motorcycle helmet some time ago, so she is applying for a weekend part-time driving instructor position in this driving school to earn back some of the money.
35:Her job interview is tomorrow, and she wants to find a student to practice with before the formal interview so that she can improve her trial lesson plan based on the feedback. And I'm the "lucky" student she chose.
37:{character:player}...Indeed, I didn't even hesitate before I signed up. As expected of a plan devised by an editor-in-chief who is proficient in communication studies...
38:{character:Nadeshiko}Hehe, facts prove that it's quite effective at selecting a candidate. Take this and sign here.
39:Nadeshiko pushes a trial lesson registration form she got from the front desk of the driving school to me.
40:{character:player}Wait! Don't I have the option to refuse?
41:{character:Nadeshiko}{var:player_nickname}, are you thinking of saying no?
42:Nadeshiko smiles as she looks me straight in the eyes.
43:...Actually, I don't think I will. Although I didn't expect I would end up taking lessons this weekend, I won't turn you down since you asked me for help, Nadeshiko.
44:I stop joking, take the pen, sign my name, and solemnly hand it back to her.
45:{character:player}Although I'm not sure how much help I'll be, I'll do my best, Instructor Nadeshiko.
46:{character:Nadeshiko}...Haha! ...You're quite motivated, aren't you? Well, {var:player_nickname}, welcome to the world of motorcycles.
47: In One Han City, a motorcycle driving test is divided into two parts: a written test and a practical assessment. We'll do the training for the written test first.
48:Nadeshiko and I are the only two people in the large classroom.
49:Nadeshiko walks up to the podium with the lesson plan she has prepared and starts to give a lecture gracefully. In this session, the instructor needs to teach students the function of various parts of a motorcycle and help students learn the traffic laws of One Han City.
50:{character:Nadeshiko}Roads are countless, but safety is priceless, so drive safely or they all lead to sadness. Although speed can be thrilling, don't forget the traffic rules while driving...
51:From the very moment the trial lecture starts, it was clear to me that Nadeshiko has done her homework as she delivered the opening remarks without checking the notes even once, and then fluently explained the basic traffic rules of One Han City and the functions of each part of a motorcycle with intimate knowledge. However, I started to feel a little... uncomfortable as I listened to her.
52:{character:player}Wait! Instructor!
53:{character:Nadeshiko}Hmm? Is there something you don't understand?
54:{character:player}What's that engine knocking failure you just mentioned?
55:{character:Nadeshiko}You just need to pay attention to whether you hear a "clunking" sound from the engine when idling.
56:{character:player}What do you mean by "idling?"
57:{character:Nadeshiko}Simply put, this is the lowest stable rotational speed of an engine when there is no load.
58:{character:player}And what do you mean by "no load?"
59:{character:Nadeshiko}Does this need to be explained?!
60:{character:player}It does...?
61:After I, a complete motorcycle novice, asked about several things in extreme detail, Nadeshiko finally notices the gap in our understanding of motorcycles. She begins to consciously adjust the rhythm of her lecture and breaks down the lecture content into smaller parts.
62:The lecture that was completely confusing to me has finally become something even a beginner could understand. Before I knew it, I'm actually engrossed in the lecture.
63:*Ringing* Only when Nadeshiko's alarm rings do I realize that the one-hour lecture has already ended.
64:{character:Nadeshiko}As expected, there is a lot to adjust. {var:player_nickname}, your feedback has been very helpful to me.
65:{character:player}Ahaha, I just showed you how an ordinary beginner would react. I'm glad you find it helpful.
67:Nadeshiko pushes a laptop in front of me.
68:{character:player}...What's this?
69:{character:Nadeshiko}This is a database of simulated questions for the official driver's license test. It's great for helping test-takers pass the official test. Try your best at answering the questions!
70:{character:player}Wait! Do I need to go so far for a test lesson? Why don't we just skip this part?
71:{character:Nadeshiko}Although this is just a test lesson, you're my first student, so I need to take full responsibility for you. Your results in the test paper are also an important indicator for testing the quality of the training!
72:{character:player}But... But...
73:{character:Nadeshiko}It's just around one hundred questions. Is that difficult enough to make you quit?
74:...I can't believe she's goading me into doing it.
75:I guess I might as well go all the way and help Nadeshiko test the quality of her teaching.
76:A feeling of pride swells up inside me when I see the words: "You have finished the required class hours for today and achieved an acceptable accuracy" on the screen.
77:{character:player}Phew! It's done. Nadeshiko...
78:As I'm about to inform Nadeshiko, I turn around to see her sitting behind the podium, fully focused on revising the PPT on her tablet and portable keyboard and completely unaware of my voice.
79:Looking at how seriously she is working, I stop talking and decide to wait for her to finish.
80:After pressing one last button, Nadeshiko finally breaks free of her state of concentration. She breathes a sigh of relief and looks surprised after she turns to look at me.
81:{character:Nadeshiko}{var:player_nickname}, has it been long since you finished answering the questions?
82:{character:player}Well, it's only been an hour or so. Have you finished revising the teaching plan?
83:{character:Nadeshiko}Geez... I was only thinking of making some basic amendments while you were answering questions, but I ended up finishing it all without realizing it... Did you not call out to me on purpose?
84:{character:player}I can't help it. Nadeshiko, you looked so focused I couldn't bear to disturb you.
85:Nadeshiko makes a stunned expression, then gives me a helpless look.
86:{character:Nadeshiko}...You're such a stealthy person. I didn't notice you staring at me at all.
87:...This must be a verbal attack used to cover up her true feelings!
88:{character:player}Alright, instead of fixating on this, why don't we make the most of our time. I remember you mentioned there is also a practical assessment? I'm really looking forward to that part!
89:{character:Nadeshiko}...Um, that's right. Next up is the practical assessment. I've prepared two training grounds for this test. One is outdoors, and the other is outdoors. You can decide which one to use.
90:Which one should I choose?
91:Although I really want to participate, I already have a motorcycle license, so I can only watch from the comment section.
92:After replying to Nadeshiko, I decide to go play some mahjong at a dojo and have a good time on the weekend using my skills.
93:*Bzzz* —My mobile phone starts vibrating just as I finish packing up.
94:It's Nadeshiko? I switch to video call in a hurry.
95:{character:player}Happy weekend, Nadeshiko.
96:{character:Nadeshiko}Happy weekend, {var:player_nickname}. Weren't you curious about the prize? It's such a hassle to leave a message, so I'll tell you like this.
97:I see. Like always, Nadeshiko is always so fast to get things done!
98:I finally understand what's happening after Nadeshiko explains everything to me.
99:Nadeshiko accidentally exceeded her living expense budget because she bought a collector's limited-edition motorcycle helmet some time ago, so she is applying for a weekend part-time driving instructor position in a driving school to earn back some of the money.
100:Her job interview is tomorrow, and she wants to find a student to practice with before the formal interview so that she can improve her trial lesson plan based on the feedback.
101:{character:player}So the mystery prize is to become Nadeshiko's student for a test lesson!
103:{character:player}Yes, I decided to make it a mystery prize because I knew it would make people curious, and I wanted to attract those who don't meet my requirements too.
104:{character:Nadeshiko}It's a shame that this prize still doesn't have a winner.
105:{character:player}Eh? Was nobody suitable for your test class even though so many people signed up?
106:{character:Nadeshiko}Well, there were quite a few suitable candidates, but they all rejected the prize after I called them to tell them the situation. They volunteered to give the prize to the next lucky winner... How mean.
107:{character:player}Pfft... It's pretty obvious why they don't have a motorcycle license then.
108:{character:Nadeshiko}Forget it. It's their loss that they'll never understand how fun it is to ride a motorcycle. Now that I've explained everything to you, let's forget about this failed draw completely. I need to think of other ways to find a suitable student. See ya.
109:{character:player}Nadeshiko, wait!
110:{character:Nadeshiko}What's up?
111:I can see that you have some trouble finding a suitable student. So I've got an idea for you.
112:{character:player}If it's just a simulation exercise to adjust your training plan, why don't you lower your selection requirements?
113:{character:Nadeshiko}What do you mean?
114:{character:player}Even if your test subject can ride a motorcycle, they can still theoretically provide feedback on your lesson plan, right? You'll also be able to gain some experience of teaching a formal class, which will make your lesson plan more reasonable.
115:{character:Nadeshiko}Wouldn't the experience not be immersive enough?
116:{character:player}There shouldn't be a problem if your student wants to help you improve your plan, is a good communicator, can interact normally, and is willing to provide feedback.
117:After considering her situation carefully, I give her my suggestion.
118:{character:Nadeshiko}I see...
119:Nadeshiko is lost in thought for a few seconds at the other end of the video, then she presses her phone a few times. After a second, the coordinates of an address appear in the dialog box of our conversation.
120:{character:Nadeshiko}If you put it that way, {var:player_nickname}, there's no better candidate than you.
121:{character:player}Huh?
